# small-business-network-packet-tracer
Packet Tracer lab: VLANs, router-on-a-stick, DHCP, NAT and ACL
# Small Office Network in Packet Tracer

I built a small office network from scratch: four VLANs, one router, one switch, a pretend ISP, and a rule that keeps Sales out of the server room. It took a few typos and one very empty NAT table, but it works.

![Topology](screenshots/topology.png)

## The setup

| VLAN | Name | Subnet | Gateway |
|---|---|---|---|
| 10 | STAFF | 192.168.10.0/24 | 192.168.10.1 |
| 20 | SALES | 192.168.20.0/24 | 192.168.20.1 |
| 30 | SERVERS | 192.168.30.0/24 | 192.168.30.1 |
| 99 | MGMT | 192.168.99.0/24 | 192.168.99.1 |

The ISP link is 203.0.113.0/30 (R1 is .1, ISP is .2), and a loopback of 8.8.8.8 on the ISP router plays the part of the internet.

## What it does

- One trunk carries all VLANs from the switch to R1, which routes between them (router on a stick).
- DHCP hands out addresses to Staff and Sales. The server keeps a fixed one (192.168.30.10).
- A default route plus NAT overload lets every private address share R1's single public one.
- An ACL blocks Sales from the server subnet but lets them online.
